  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \n\nA low-pressure system traveling northeast along the Canada/New \nEngland border will continue to drag a cold front across the \nmiddle Ohio Valley and Central Appalachians today. Mainly rain\nand gusty winds have been accompanying the frontal passage,\nthough some thunderstorms could also develop this afternoon.\nWhile the severe potential for today is low, a stronger gust or\ntwo could be possible with storms this afternoon. Additionally,\ngiven wet ground from previous rain, even sub-severe wind gusts\nmay down trees. \n\nCold air advection will bring a noticeable drop in temperatures \nbehind the front, and is anticipated to sustain breezy conditions \ninto Saturday. Precipitation starts tapering off from west to \neast once the axis of an upper trough passes overhead tonight. \nIn the higher elevations of the northeast mountains, rain may \ntransition to a wintry mix before precipitation comes to an end\nSaturday morning. Snow is not expected to accumulate.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nDry air advecting into the region during the day on Saturday\nshould yield widespread dew point values below 32F Saturday\nevening into Sunday morning. While some modest flow with cold\nadvection may continue on the south side of the high heading\ninto early Sunday morning, think at least valleys in the Metro\nValley and southern coalfields will decouple sufficiently to \nradiate out to the freezing mark for a few hours heading into\ndaybreak. Have expanded the Freeze Watch to cover the balance of\nour counties for Sunday morning. If coverage of freezing\ntemperatures is insufficient to close the growing season,\nadditional frost/freeze highlights are possible for Monday\nmorning.\n\nAs of 138 PM...\n\nHigh pressure and drier air build in from the west on Saturday, \nbringing an end to any light precipitation lingering over the \nnortheast mountains. Breezy conditions are expected to gradually \nrelax throughout the day, then calm and clear conditions set in for \nSaturday night. \n\nTemperatures remain below normal through the day, then overnight \nlows dive down into the mid 20s to 30s. A Freeze Watch has been \nissued for portions of the mountains, northern West Virginia, \nsouthwest Virginia, and southeast Ohio, where there is highest \nconfidence in below freezing temperatures developing late Saturday \nnight into early Sunday morning. Frost Advisories are likely to be \nneeded for the remainder of the area. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nHigh pressure is expected to sustain drier weather from Sunday \nthrough at least the middle of next week while a low spirals off of \nthe Virginia/Carolina coast. Although temperatures will trend warmer \nduring this time, one more frosty morning could be possible on \nMonday.",
